---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import MoreTools from '../../components/MoreTools.astro';
import { site } from '../../site.config';
import { articleLd, faqLd, howToLd } from '../../lib/seo';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/url';

const title = 'Do QR Codes Expire?';
const description = 'No — the QR pattern itself never expires. What can stop working is a dynamic-QR/shortener subscription behind it — here is how to tell which one you have.';
const updated = site.updated;
const published = '2026-09-23';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Do QR codes expire after a certain time?', a: 'The pattern itself does not have a timer or expiry date built in — QR is just an open encoding standard (ISO/IEC 18004). A printed static code will scan for as long as it stays physically legible, which can be years or decades.' },
  { q: 'Then why do some QR codes stop working?', a: 'Because they were generated by a "free" QR code service as a <em>dynamic</em> code: instead of your content, the pattern stores a short link to that company\'s server, which redirects each scan to your real destination. If you stop paying, the account is deleted, or the company shuts down, that redirect breaks — the printed pattern is unchanged, but it now leads nowhere.' },
  { q: 'How do I know if a code I already printed is static or dynamic?', a: `Scan it with any QR reader and look at the decoded text. If it shows your actual content (a full URL to your own site, Wi-Fi details, a vCard…) it is static. If it shows a short link on a domain like qrco.de, qr-code-generator.com, qrfy.com or similar, it is dynamic and depends on that provider. You can also use our <a href="${withBase('/check/')}">QR code checker</a> to classify it automatically.` },
  { q: 'Is a dynamic QR code ever the right choice?', a: 'Yes — if you genuinely need to redirect the same printed code to a different URL later (e.g. change a menu link without reprinting signage) or want scan analytics, a dynamic code trades permanence for that flexibility. Just budget for the subscription indefinitely, and pick a provider you trust to stay in business.' },
];
---

<Base
  title={title}
  description={description}
  image="/og/guide/do-qr-codes-expire.png"
  jsonLd={[
    articleLd({ headline: title, description, url: '/guide/do-qr-codes-expire/', datePublished: published, dateModified: updated }),
    faqLd(faqs.map((f) => ({ q: f.q, a: f.a.replace(/<[^>]+>/g, '') }))),
    howToLd({
      name: 'How to make sure your QR code cannot expire',
      description: 'Three checks that guarantee a QR code you print will keep working indefinitely, with no third-party service in between.',
      steps: [
        { name: 'Use a generator that encodes your content directly', text: 'Choose a generator (like this one) that encodes your actual content directly — check that the decoded payload is your real URL/text/Wi-Fi data, not a redirect domain.' },
        { name: 'Check a code you already printed', text: 'If you already printed a code, run it through our checker to see exactly what it contains.' },
        { name: 'Link to a URL you control', text: 'For a link, point it at a URL you control rather than a third-party shortener, so it depends only on your own site staying up.' },
      ],
    }),
  ]}
>
  <article class="container container--narrow section prose">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ name: 'Home', url: '/' }, { name: 'Guides', url: '/guide/' }, { name: title, url: '/guide/do-qr-codes-expire/' }]} />
    <p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="muted small">Last updated: {updated}</p>

    <p>
      <strong>No — a QR code's black-and-white pattern never expires.</strong> QR is an open, royalty-free encoding
      standard (Denso Wave holds the patent but has chosen not to exercise it); once printed, the pattern is just
      ink on a surface and will scan for as long as it stays legible.
      What people usually mean by "my QR code expired" is that a <em>dynamic</em> QR code's underlying redirect
      link stopped working because a subscription lapsed — the code looks identical, but the service behind it shut
      the door.
    </p>

    <h2>Static vs dynamic: the actual difference</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th></th><th>Static QR code</th><th>Dynamic QR code</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>What's inside the pattern</td><td>Your actual content (URL, Wi-Fi, text…)</td><td>A short link to the provider's server</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Can the destination change?</td><td>No — generate a new code</td><td>Yes, from the provider's dashboard</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Depends on a third party?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, for as long as you use it</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Can it "expire"?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, if the subscription lapses or the service shuts down</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scan analytics</td><td>Not built in</td><td>Usually included</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Full comparison: <a href={withBase('/guide/static-vs-dynamic-qr-codes/')}>static vs dynamic QR codes</a>.</p>

    <h2>Why "free" QR generators often create dynamic codes</h2>
    <p>
      Many QR-generator websites default to a dynamic code even for a simple URL, because it lets them offer a free
      tier and then charge once you've printed thousands of flyers and can't easily switch providers. Trustpilot is
      full of reviews from people whose codes stopped scanning after a trial period ended — the QR code review site
      itself didn't change, the redirect it depended on did.
    </p>

    <h2>How to make sure your QR code cannot expire</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Choose a generator (like this one) that encodes your actual content directly — check that the decoded payload is your real URL/text/Wi-Fi data, not a redirect domain.</li>
      <li>If you already printed a code, run it through our <a href={withBase('/check/')}>checker</a> to see exactly what it contains.</li>
      <li>For a link, point it at a URL you control rather than a third-party shortener, so it depends only on your own site staying up.</li>
    </ol>

    <Faq items={faqs} schema={false} />
  </article>

  <section class="container">
    <MoreTools />
  </section>
</Base>
